README.md:46:The repository contains Entire configuration in `.entire/settings.json` with checkpoint mode `git-refs` and telemetry disabled. Scoped development-session imports and checkpoint delivery have been verified for the analytical-agent commits. For future local sessions, check:
README.md:54:Git configuration alone does not prove that checkpoints have been recorded. Follow the [EHL Entire event instructions](https://hack.entire.io/ehl-munich-2026/) for event-specific requirements.
README.md:71:`hollerau.analytical` uses `gpt-6-astra` for a negotiator and a separately prompted final decision-maker. The v7 controller gives Astra the last word on counters, questions, confirmation, acceptance and walking away, including after supplier agreement and near wave deadlines. Python cannot override that selection with its uncertain acceptance model.
README.md:75:V7 preserves original should-cost evidence, separates production-cost estimates from acceptance behaviour, and calibrates supplier economics using completed practice cost breakdowns. It includes higher-price sustainability alternatives and explicit downside comparisons. See [the implementation and assumptions](docs/evaluation/v7-supplier-economics.md). No local tests are run for this update.
README.md:77:V7's two hosted runs were mixed: **P02 improved to 0.255** (previous best 0.157), while **P18 scored 0** after Astra countered a final offer and the supplier walked away (previous best 0.340). P02's relationship-cost forecast error fell to €150 on the observed deal. See [the actual v7 results and remaining weaknesses](docs/evaluation/v7-arena-results.md), [the v6 P02 diagnosis](docs/evaluation/v6-p02-arena.md), and [the earlier hosted evaluation](docs/evaluation/v51-arena-results.md). Future strategy evaluations use **P02 and P18 in Arena only**, with local tests stopped.
README.md:79:See [setup, parameters and current decision flow](docs/ANALYTICAL.md), [initial five-session results](docs/evaluation/FIVE_PRACTICE.md), and [improved five-session comparison](docs/evaluation/IMPROVED_FIVE_PRACTICE.md). `play` and `run` default to `--brain analytical`; the legacy agents remain explicitly selectable. Use `tacto-bayes` for bounded practice. `local` retains its model-free default.
README.md:81:The implementation uses Entire's supported external-agent import to attach actual scoped Codex development records. Earlier unrelated history and credentials are excluded; original source-session IDs, timestamps and record hashes are preserved. See the provenance section of the setup guide. Commit trailers and remote checkpoint refs are verified after publishing.
docs/ANALYTICAL.md:1:# Analytical buyer agent — astra-v7-supplier-economics
docs/ANALYTICAL.md:5:V7 adds independent supplier economics, original cost evidence in both model calls, completed-practice cost calibration and higher-price sustainability packages. See [the current assumptions and evaluation scope](evaluation/v7-supplier-economics.md). The former quote-implied production-cost mixture described in the historical sections below no longer drives the Astra path. Current strategy evaluation is hosted P02/P18 only; local tests are stopped.
docs/ANALYTICAL.md:17:Hosted runs require their own authorized session allowance. SQLite reserves starts before HTTP; failed or ambiguous starts still count. Do not change the run ID or database to bypass a cap. There are no automatic replacements. V6 development used saved-input replays of P02/P18 without calling Arena. See [validation](evaluation/v6-final-authority.md).
docs/ANALYTICAL.md:34:Both roles use `gpt-6-astra`, with separate requests and no negotiator rationale passed to the final decision-maker. The reviewer sees the evidence, current and historical packages, direct cost, expected full cost including relationship penalties, its component breakdown, and the same weighted cost range used by the proposer. It receives no Python winner to rubber-stamp. The first call's proposals are suggestions; the final call chooses action/package IDs. Python validates IDs, the actual current standing quote, exact options and must-haves, then renders the approved terms. It does not rerank or repair the final selection silently.
docs/ANALYTICAL.md:42:Verified numeric arguments are selectable by evidence ID and rendered from the facts used to compute them. There is no blanket digit ban on these arguments or structured terms. Unverified free-text numeric claims are not sent. The current message wording uses respectful templates with an Astra-selected tone and targeted question; arbitrary model-written factual claims are not part of the final action schema.
docs/ANALYTICAL.md:57:| `b_capacity` | Sensitivity to volume above the latest offered allocation |
docs/ANALYTICAL.md:74:The remainder of this section documents the explicit `--primary qwen --reviewer primary` or model-free comparison path. Its Python ranking and closing rules do **not** control the default v6 Astra path above.
docs/ANALYTICAL.md:76:Enumerate reference and one-field changes plus up to 600 complete packages from the starter kit. Search a price grid from 20% below to 15% above the current quote, with additional prices derived from estimated supplier value and a concession increment. Prices remain within a bounded exploration range. Premiums can buy better non-price terms. This is a bounded heuristic, not a global optimiser.
docs/ANALYTICAL.md:96:The shared move budget is 35 seconds starting when `LoggedTable` receives the supplier reply, before attachment downloads. V6 reserves time for final authorization: the proposal call gets at most 14 seconds of HTTP phases under its shortened deadline, and the final call at most 20, further capped by the remaining window minus three seconds. Attachment phases use at most eight seconds and share the clock. There is no large probabilistic grid search in v6. The practice runner checks the accepted ID against the last received standing ID without an extra network refresh; the server validates it atomically on submission.
docs/ANALYTICAL.md:100:The final decision-maker runs on every v6 decision, independently of whether the first model succeeded. It chooses which supplier to contact, the action, up to three complete packages, a supported question, evidence and tone. Buyer constraints remain mandatory. The same decision contract applies when Qwen proposes and Astra is selected as final reviewer.
docs/ANALYTICAL.md:118:The private v4 work uses the current private v3 implementation as its frozen baseline. Earlier inspected cases are development data. Its target is a mean score of at least 0.90 on a separately generated evaluation set; the gate also requires valid actions and reports every zero and regression. The simulator, opponents and score function are unchanged. No hidden supplier parameters enter the buyer. Full results and source fingerprints remain under ignored `hollerau/logs/private-v4/`; a development score alone does not pass this gate. Qwen and Astra are disabled in these score experiments, which measure the Python decision policy only.
docs/ANALYTICAL.md:122:The offline baseline comparison uses identical simulator cases and seeds with models disabled. It tests decision mechanics; it does not reproduce the hosted supplier or validate Qwen extraction. Hosted comparisons use the same five case IDs but different stochastic supplier sessions. Several changes ship together, so score differences cannot identify which component caused them. The second batch encountered an HTTP 422 when accepting an indicative P10 alternative. P10 was recovered on its registered offer without opening another session. The v2.1 closing guard was added before P11; the report marks this intervention rather than presenting all five as uninterrupted autonomous runs. Historical architecture v3 images represent the earlier proposal; the flow above describes the current implementation.
